<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalmyks</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kalmyk people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kalmyks are a Mongolic people descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a>, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghuts</a>, who migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in the early 17th century and founded the Kalmyk Khanate under Russian suzerainty. In 1771 a large part of them, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubashi-khan">Ubashi Khan</a>, set out back to Dzungaria, a journey in which many died. Those who remained live mainly in the Republic of Kalmykia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the only region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> is the traditional religion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalmyks.jpg" alt="Kalmyks" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bata Badmayevich Erdneyev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kalmykia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early seventeenth century the Oirats were divided and under pressure from the Khalkha Mongols and the Kazakhs; some groups migrated west to the Volga, where they became the <b>Kalmyks</b>, and others moved to the Kokonor region and Tibet as the Khoshut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dzungar invasion of 1723 drove the Kazakhs from their eastern pastures and from the Syr Darya towns, and although they recovered some ground, fear of the Dzungars and pressure from the <b>Kalmyks</b>, Bashkirs and Cossacks led them to seek protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The darker legacy includes the Red Terror, collectivisation, the Gulag, the Great Terror of 1937–1938 and the deportation of whole peoples such as the Chechens, <b>Kalmyks</b>, Crimean Tatars and Volga Germans during the Second World War, whose consequences still shape relations in the North Caucasus and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1801 the Russian emperor Paul I allowed the sultan Bukey, a son of Nurali Khan of the Junior Juz, to lead some 5,000 families across the Ural River into the steppe between the Ural and the Volga left empty by the <b>Kalmyk</b> departure of 1771.</blockquote><a href="/state/bukey-horde">Bukey Horde</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Kalmyks</b> were pastoral nomads following Tibetan Buddhism, with monasteries and a code of law based on the Mongol-Oirat Code of 1640.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalmyk-khanate">Kalmyk Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
